## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical CV pipeline using lightweight, non-ML methods for a common task.

## Key Concepts
- Circle detection with Hough transform
- Radius-based grouping for coin types
- Noise/shadow handling with blur and histogram equalization
- Validation with synthetic data

## Code Example
```javascript
// OpenCV.js-like pseudocode
let src = cv.imread('table.jpg');
cv.cvtColor(src, src, cv.COLOR_RGBA2GRAY);
cv.equalizeHist(src, src);
cv.GaussianBlur(src, new cv.Size(9,9), 2);
let circles = new cv.Mat();
cv.HoughCircles(src, circles, cv.HOUGH_GRADIENT, 1.2, 20, 50, 30, 10, 60);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you address perspective distortions?
- How would you validate robustness with lighting changes?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design production-ready, on-device face verification systems with robust anti-spoofing capabilities under strict computational constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge-optimized inference architectures
- 3D head pose estimation from facial landmarks
- Temporal liveness detection for spoof resistance
- Parameter-efficient 5-shot adaptation via adapter layers
- Hybrid synthetic-to-real data generation strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: unified inference pipeline
function processFrame(frame) {
  const backbone = extractFeatures(frame);
  const detection = faceDetectionHead(backbone);
  const liveness = temporalLivenessHead(backbone);
  return { detection, liveness };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a complete edge-CV pipeline: real-time monocular 3D pose with per-object velocity, occlusion robustness, and rapid on-device adaptation to unseen parts. Emphasizes practical data strategies and evaluation in an industrial setting.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 3D pose estimation
- Per-instance velocity prediction for planning
- On-device few-shot adaptation (5-shot)
- Occlusion handling and data fusion strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: forward pass with adaptation trigger
function forward(frame, exemplars){
  let features = backbone(frame);
  let detections = detector(features);
  let poses = poseHead(features, detections);
  let vel = velocityHead(features, detections, previous);
  if (exemplars) adapt(poses, exemplars);
  return {detections, poses, vel};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify robustness to occlusion in your evaluation?
- What ablation would you run to isolate the velocity head contribution?
